# Target C-II — an unbounded reflection family survives to near-quarter-power contact depth

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only corollary of the geometric contact-tube theorem; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** Type-II full-core `eta=3/26` reflection branch  
**Dependencies:** slow-scale simultaneous reflection family; geometric contact-tube packing  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_near_quarter_contact_depth_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The geometric contact-tube theorem allows a stronger tradeoff than keeping the full slow-scale family. By thinning the number of reflection modules, the isolated contact radius around each surviving module can be raised to almost the inverse-quarter geometric scale itself.

Let `omega_D` be any function satisfying

\[
\boxed{
\omega_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\omega_D=o(D^{1/8}).
}
\tag{QD.1}
\]

Set

\[
\boxed{
\kappa_D:={D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2}.
}
\tag{QD.2}
\]

Then `kappa_D->infinity` and `kappa_D=o(D^(1/4))`, so the slow-scale reflection theorem applies. It produces at least

\[
(\rho_*-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
=(\rho_*-o(1))\omega_D^2
\tag{QD.3}
\]

pairwise-separated simultaneous reflection modules.

Choose only

\[
\boxed{m_D:=\lfloor\omega_D\rfloor}
\tag{QD.4}
\]

of them, and set the contact-buffer radius

\[
\boxed{r_D:=\lfloor\kappa_D\rfloor.}
\tag{QD.5}
\]

For sufficiently large `D`, (QD.3) is much larger than (QD.4), so this thinning is available. The resulting `m_D` modules satisfy simultaneously:

\[
\boxed{m_D\to\infty,}
\tag{QD.6}
\]

\[
\boxed{
r_D
=(1-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2},
}
\tag{QD.7}
\]

\[
\boxed{
N_{r_D}(U_i)\cap N_{r_D}(U_j)=\varnothing
\quad(i\ne j),
}
\tag{QD.8}
\]

with no unit-contact edge between distinct buffers, and

\[
\boxed{
\left|\bigcup_{i=1}^{m_D}N_{r_D}(U_i)\right|
=O(D/\omega_D)=o(D).
}
\tag{QD.9}

Thus an unbounded number of independent reflection modules can each be protected through contact-graph radius `D^(1/4-o(1))` while their entire buffered population stays sublinear.

For example, choosing

\[
\omega_D=\log\log D
\tag{QD.10}
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
\log\log D+O(1)
}
\]

independent modules, each with isolated contact radius

\[
\boxed{
{D^{1/4}\over(\log\log D)^2}(1-o(1)),
}
\tag{QD.11}

while all buffered modules together occupy only

\[
\boxed{O(D/\log\log D)}
\tag{QD.12}

source points.

---

## 1. Availability of `omega_D` surviving modules

From (QD.2),

\[
{D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}=\omega_D^2.
\]

The slow-scale family therefore has size

\[
(\rho_*-o(1))\omega_D^2.
\tag{QD.13}
\]

Since `rho_*>0` is fixed and `omega_D->infinity`,

\[
(\rho_*-o(1))\omega_D^2
>\omega_D
\tag{QD.14}
\]

for sufficiently large `D`. Hence one may choose exactly `m_D=floor(omega_D)` modules without affecting any of the pairwise geometric separation statements inherited from the slow-scale extraction.

---

## 2. Contact-tube cost at radius `r_D=kappa_D`

The geometric tube theorem gives, for one module and any integer `r`,

\[
|N_r(U_i)|
\le
32C_A(r+h_*+1/2)D^{3/4}
+32(r+h_*+1/2)^2,
\tag{QD.15}
\]

where `C_A=9.56283745460...` and `h_*<0.335` are fixed.

Take `r=r_D=floor(kappa_D)`. Then

\[
|N_{r_D}(U_i)|
=O(D^{3/4}\kappa_D+\kappa_D^2).
\tag{QD.16}
\]

Summing over `m_D=O(omega_D)` selected modules,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\left|\bigcup_{i=1}^{m_D}N_{r_D}(U_i)\right|
&\le
O\left(
\omega_DD^{3/4}{D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2}
\right)\\
&\quad+
O\left(
\omega_D{D^{1/2}\over\omega_D^4}
\right)\\
&=
O(D/\omega_D)
+O(D^{1/2}/\omega_D^3).
\end{aligned}
\tag{QD.17}
\]

The second term is smaller, and (QD.9) follows.

---

## 3. The near-quarter buffers remain disjoint

The slow-scale module separation at the choice (QD.2) is

\[
\operatorname{dist}_{\mathbb R^2}(U_i,U_j)
=\Omega(\kappa_DD^{3/4})
=\Omega\left({D\over\omega_D^2}\right).
\tag{QD.18}
\]

Every unit-contact path has Euclidean displacement at most its number of edges, so the contact-graph distance between distinct modules obeys the same lower bound.

But

\[
2r_D+1
=O\left({D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2}\right)
=o\left({D\over\omega_D^2}\right).
\tag{QD.19}
\]

Therefore no two radius-`r_D` contact neighborhoods intersect or have a contact edge between them. This proves (QD.8).

---

## 4. Slow choices give `D^(1/4-o(1))` contact depth

If `omega_D=D^{o(1)}` in addition to (QD.1), then

\[
r_D
={D^{1/4}\over\omega_D^2}(1-o(1))
=D^{1/4-o(1)}.
\tag{QD.20}
\]

At the same time `m_D=omega_D+O(1)->infinity`. Hence the reflection survivor contains an unbounded family of modules for which any contact-based obstruction must propagate through `D^(1/4-o(1))` graph layers before it can reach another selected module or leave the prescribed isolated buffer.

The concrete `omega_D=log log D` choice gives (QD.11)--(QD.12).

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The remaining reflection obstruction is now local in a strong sense. One can retain an unbounded number of simultaneous tooth flips, and around every corresponding blocker path system expose a disjoint contact neighborhood of near-quarter-power radius. The union of all these very deep buffers is still `o(D)`.

This does not itself show that a contact component terminates. A source contact chain can have length `D^(1/4-o(1))`. But the burden on the surviving escape is now explicit: for every one of `omega_D` independent modules, a repair obstruction must propagate through a near-quarter-power chain while staying inside the narrow antipodal geometric tube from the blocker-shell theorem.

The next useful statement would couple graph depth to geometry: prove that any unit-contact path of length comparable to `D^(1/4)/omega_D^2` launched from the thin blocker shell either exits the allowed support-normal tube, accumulates enough turn to consume a non-negligible part of the global contact-turn budget, or reaches a vertex with a feasible inward first-order motion. Any one of these outcomes would convert the current deep-buffer normal form into an actual module repair or a global contradiction.

**Disposition:** by thinning the module count, raised the independent contact-buffer radius from `o(kappa_D)` to `kappa_D` itself. For subpolynomial `omega_D`, an unbounded family survives with `D^(1/4-o(1))` contact depth and only `o(D)` total buffered mass. C-II and Level I remain open.
